{0 JavaScript interop}

These modules expose [of_js] and [to_js] to cross the JavaScript boundary:
{!Workero.Request}, {!Workero.Response}, {!Workero.Stream},
{!Workero.Form_data}, {!Workero.Websocket}, {!Workero.Env}, {!Workero.Context},
{!Workero.Scheduled}, {!Workero.Cf}, {!Workero.KV}, {!Workero.R2}, {!Workero.D1},
{!Workero.Sql_value} and {!Workero.Promise}. Check each linked signature for its
conversion types:
{!Workero.Promise.of_js}, for example, requires a decoder. Other modules need
not expose a pair. {!Workero.URL} parses from a string and has no
[of_js]/[to_js] pair. Use the supported conversions to call a JavaScript API
that workero does not wrap.

{!Workero.js_value} is the type of a raw, unchecked JavaScript value. It is the
same type as [Js_of_ocaml.Js.Unsafe.any]. Module signatures show the jsoo name.
Use [Js_of_ocaml.Js] to read fields, call methods and convert strings.
Workero already wraps timers, [Date], random-value functions, and [console]. See
{{!page-globals}Timers, time, and randomness} and {{!page-logging}Logging}.

{@ocaml[
open Js_of_ocaml

let global name = Js.Unsafe.get Js.Unsafe.global (Js.string name)

let handler req _env _ctx =
  let raw : Workero.js_value = Workero.Request.to_js req in
  let method_ : Js.js_string Js.t = Js.Unsafe.get raw (Js.string "method") in
  let done_ : string Workero.Promise.t =
    Workero.Promise.of_js ~decode:Workero.Decode.string
      (Js.Unsafe.meth_call (global "Promise") "resolve"
         [| Js.Unsafe.inject (Js.string "done") |])
  in
  let word = Workero.Promise.await done_ in
  Workero.Response.text (Js.to_string method_ ^ " " ^ word ^ "\n")

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

{!Workero.Promise.of_js} takes a [~decode] function. It turns the resolved
JavaScript value into an OCaml value. If [decode] raises, the Promise rejects
and {!Workero.Promise.await} re-raises that exception, for example
[Invalid_argument "Workero.Decode: expected string, got number"].

{1 Decode}

{!Workero.Decode} has decoders for raw values: [js] (undecoded), [unit],
[string], [int], [float], [bool], [bytes], [option], [list] and
[field name decoder]. A decoder is a function from {!Workero.js_value}. It raises
[Invalid_argument] with the expected and the actual JavaScript type when the
value does not fit. [int] also rejects a number with a fraction or outside the
range of [int]. [bytes] rejects array elements outside 0..255. [field] reads
one property of an object or a function:

{@ocaml[
open Js_of_ocaml

let global name = Js.Unsafe.get Js.Unsafe.global (Js.string name)

let user =
  let raw =
    Js.Unsafe.obj
      [|
        ("name", Js.Unsafe.inject (Js.string "ada"));
        ("age", Js.Unsafe.inject 36);
      |]
  in
  Workero.Promise.of_js
    ~decode:(fun v -> Workero.Decode.(field "name" string v, field "age" int v))
    (Js.Unsafe.meth_call (global "Promise") "resolve" [| raw |])

let handler _req _env _ctx =
  let name, age = Workero.Promise.await user in
  Workero.Response.text (Printf.sprintf "%s is %d\n" name age)

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

Add [js_of_ocaml] to [libraries] next to [workero].
